The Logic: Four Levers and One Backbone
Lever	Question it answers	Phases
Affordability	Can people pay for healthy food?	2
Access	Can people reach it easily?	1, 3
Ability	Do people have the time and skills to cook it?	4
Environment	Is the healthy choice the visible, default one?	5
Accountability (backbone)	Does the system measure and correct itself?	6
The Six Phases
Phase	Timeline	Core actions	Output
1. Local Diagnosis	Months 1–3	Map food outlets by neighborhood (fresh vs. ultra-processed). Compare price per calorie and per nutrient for a healthy basket vs. an ultra-processed one. Identify areas with the highest diabetes, obesity, and anemia rates. Publish all findings openly.	Public report with a neighborhood food-access map
2. Make Nutritious Food Cheaper	Months 3–12	Redirect subsidies from industrial crops to fruits, vegetables, legumes, and small farmers. Tax sugary drinks and ultra-processed foods, with revenue earmarked for the program. Zero VAT on locally produced fresh produce. Public procurement (schools, hospitals, prisons) prioritizes local producers.	A healthy basket with a stable price, competitive with the ultra-processed one
3. Shorten the Supply Chain	Months 6–18	Neighborhood producer markets at least twice a week. Buying cooperatives for bulk, farm-gate prices. Mobile points of sale for areas with no market. Shared logistics to cut small producers’ transport costs.	Fresh food within a 10-minute walk in most neighborhoods
4. Recover Time and Knowledge	Months 6–24	Cooking workshops in schools, community centers, and workplaces (local, quick, low-cost recipes). School meals made from real food bought from local producers. School and community gardens. Recipe books (digital and print) with cost per serving.	A generation that can cook and values local food
5. Regulate the Environment	Months 12–36	Mandatory front-of-package warning labels, adapted to each country. Ban ultra-processed food advertising aimed at children under 12. Remove ultra-processed products from vending machines and school shops. Regulate advertising in public spaces and transport.	An environment where healthy is visible and easy
6. Measure and Sustain	Ongoing	Annual indicators (see below). Citizen council of farmers, nutritionists, students, and merchants. Protected budget, independent of the governing party. Open evaluation and phase adjustments based on results.	A self-correcting system that survives political change
Indicators (Phase 6 Scorecard)
Dimension	Indicator
Affordability	Relative price of the healthy basket vs. the ultra-processed basket
Access	% of households within 10 minutes’ walk of fresh food
Consumption	Average fruit and vegetable intake
Health outcomes	Overweight prevalence; childhood anemia prevalence
Governance	Share of the budget protected; frequency of public reports
Dependencies and Sequencing
Phase 1 feeds everything. Its data decides where to prioritize markets, mobile sales points, and workshops.
Phases 2 and 3 are linked. Lower prices need shorter chains, and shorter chains need steady demand from public procurement.
Phase 2’s tax funds the program. Earmarking prevents the money from disappearing into general budgets.
Phase 5 comes later because regulation is politically harder once communities already see benefits from Phases 2–4.
Phase 6 runs from day one. Baselines come from Phase 1.
Adaptation Checklist
Local variable	What to adjust
Income level	Tax rates, subsidy size, and the price threshold for “competitive”
Food culture	Which foods and recipes count as the healthy basket
Urban vs. rural	Fixed markets vs. mobile points and cooperatives
Institutional capacity	Which regulations in Phase 5 are realistic and in what order
Main health burden	Whether to prioritize anemia, obesity, or diabetes in targeting
